cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or build on these experiments, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "ITASORL: Incidental Detection of Surrogate Worlds by a From-Scratch Digital Organism"
authors:
  - family-names: "Tate"
    given-names: "Levy"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-1337-0709"
repository-code: "https://github.com/iLevyTate/ItaSoRL"
license: MIT
version: 1.5.0
date-released: "2026-10-19"
abstract: >-
  ITASORL measures whether a recurrent agent trained from scratch, with no
  imported semantic knowledge and no reward for detection, comes to carry in its
  state whether its world runs on authentic base dynamics or a substrate-flawed
  surrogate, set against how detectable the flaw is to a privileged detector at a
  stated detector-side noise. Hand-authored flaws (L1 discretization, L2 rollout
  drift) are detectable but did not meet the encoding criterion, which was
  registered for L2 and applied to L1 after its run (the L2 prediction-only
  world model received each action one step late, so that arm was a weaker
  predictor than designed, docs/CORRECTIONS.md, 2026-10-16; retrained with the
  timing corrected it reads the same, 0.544 against 0.517 with the old timing,
  three seeds each); at the L3 gate's
  detector noise the L2 flaws read 0.62 to 0.65 on one noise draw and 0.60 and
  0.50 averaged over eight, so the matched contrast is L1 against L3. Rerun on the corrected trainer, the L1 state reads
  0.551, as before the correction; the same readout registers the seam at a
  coarser, supra-band grid (0.06), where an untrained trunk already reads 0.672,
  so the L1 negative is specific to the in-band grid. Against a learned-dynamics fingerprint (L3),
  survival-trained agents carrying a next-observation auxiliary at the
  registered 300-update budget, with the trainer corrected in October 2026,
  read 0.733 against 0.589 for a prediction-only arm, each arm on the episodes
  its own policy drives; a decoder that sees only the observations and actions
  reads 0.724. The registered rule is
  not met, because the L0 gate is open on the registered evaluation-world pair
  (0.559); the decodability clauses are met. On the same pair, survival agents
  of a second seed set read 0.561 [0.527, 0.595]. Across eight independent pairs a
  test on the signed pair means accepts equivalence to chance (p = 0.022), but
  folded about 0.5 the same draws give p = 0.129, not equivalent; the gate
  accepts on two of the eight draws individually. Without the auxiliary the arm reads 0.613 at 300 updates
  and 0.685 at 450, so the auxiliary result is bounded by budget. At matched
  evaluation input a prediction-trained trunk reads the world within about
  0.01 of the survival trunk (0.721 against 0.733): the signal rides on the trajectories the foraging policy
  produces and is kept by a trunk shaped by next-observation prediction. With
  death switched off and a pellet-directed reward in place of survival, the
  agent's state reads 0.796 and was recorded as meeting every gate, but that
  leakage gate summed the homeostatic reward rather than pellets, so full-rule
  validity was not established (docs/CORRECTIONS.md, 2026-10-12). A
  confirmation frozen before it ran scores the same trainer on matched world
  seeds: survival reads 0.800 and the pellet objective 0.843, and both runs are
  uninformative because the untrained arm reads 0.614, outside the 0.1 floor. A
  later in-fold linear sensory control, frozen after those raw outcomes were
  known, leaves 0.761 and 0.780 with the untrained arm at 0.596, whose interval
  crosses 0.60 (FINDINGS section 21); survival is not required for that
  controlled decodability reading. A hand-authored drag that, like the learned
  law, depends on the agent's own velocity is read through the same direction,
  so nothing here is specific to learned dynamics. Random velocity jitter,
  read from agents raised under the learned law, reaches the 0.65 bar at no setting tried, including strongly autocorrelated jitter matched
  on its 24-step deviation, so the comparison names no mechanism (FINDINGS
  section 22.3). A common-garden control passes its frozen rule
  on the historical agents (0.666, 0.684): prefix condition
  remains decodable after restoring authentic dynamics, and on the corrected agents that is
  carried mostly by the physical footprint of the prefix, not by memory (see
  FINDINGS section 10.6.1 and 17.7). Four readouts on the saved agents
  (FINDINGS section 23) find the world direction faint in the state's own
  geometry in trained and untrained states alike; no named near feature of the
  agent's own world read beyond an observation-summary control; on the episodes
  that survived every condition, removing the decoded direction while the agent
  acts changed behavior no more than a sham removal, but a probe refit on the
  erased states, with each same-world twin pair in one fold, still reads 0.747,
  so the removal bounds what the decoded direction carries, not what the state
  carries; and decodability after a linear observation control grows across
  training snapshots (clearing the frozen 0.05 bound with the auxiliary, +0.224;
  without it +0.104, whose lower bound of 0.046 misses that bound) while the
  probe direction reorganizes.
  Corrections and what each does not establish: docs/CORRECTIONS.md.
keywords:
  - artificial-life
  - reinforcement-learning
  - self-supervised-learning
  - simulation-hypothesis
  - world-models
